Evan checked Abby’s camera.

Her camera stayed lowered.

Diane said, “Will you remain upstairs during the session?”

“No.”

“Will you remain on site?”

“No.”

He spoke reluctantly. His eyes moved toward the cabinet and away.

“I am logging out at four,” he said. “I’ll write my location, return my radio, and leave through the loading entrance. My name stays out of whatever comes after.”

Diane nodded. “I’ll record that.”

Ron bent for the filters.

“Ron.”

He paused.

“If I allow it, the end point is mine.”

“It already was.”

He carried the box down the corridor. The cart-wheel rhythm continued beyond him, then faded toward the boiler room. His keys gave one practical jingle at the junction, the heavy boiler key arriving after the others. Diane listened until the ventilation covered the sound.

Evan said, “He thinks the last case was a mistake.”

“He thinks his consent mattered.”

“It does.”

“Then stop treating his absence as a variable you can replace.”

Abby set the camera on the desk. “I have a proposal.”

Diane waited.

“One continuous source remains with me from visitor arrival through exit. No one handles that card except me, and I make the first copy under your supervision. Last time, my camera preserved the part the finished cut weakened. This time, source custody includes my right to stop recording when safety or consent requires it. Diane runs a separate recorder outside Evan’s handling. We synchronize both to the facilities clock and mark every room change. Evan can operate a second camera, but his files count as production sources, not independent corroboration.”

Evan frowned. “Why is my camera less independent?”

“Because you’re directing the session while filming it.”

“I am documenting.”

“You narrate before checks finish.”

“That keeps context.”

“It creates context.”

Diane looked at Abby’s notebook. “What about the medium?”

“No private briefing on camera or off. Before formal start, everyone states what information they already know. Names used in casual conversation get logged. Any personal claim gets matched against information participants disclosed earlier.”

Evan’s frown deepened. “You’re turning the session into a cross-examination.”

“I’m preserving its sequence.”

“You’re assuming contamination.”

“I’m documenting exposure.”

Diane took her facilities recorder from the locked desk drawer. It was a plain black unit used for incident statements, with no old files on its memory and no wireless connection. She placed it beside Abby’s camera.

“Fresh batteries,” she said. “Format the card in frame. Record the device serial number and clock.”

Abby opened her notebook to a clean page.

Evan stared at the recorder. “So you’re allowing it.”

“I am defining conditions.”

“That means yes.”

“It means you will hear the conditions before anyone enters.”

At 3:51, Diane carried a folding whiteboard from the adjacent training room and placed it against the office wall. She wrote without abbreviations.

NO STUDENT IDENTITIES.

NO DEATH CLAIMS PRESENTED AS FACT.

NO LOCKED OR SEALED AREA ACCESS.

NO PRIVATE NAMES IN RELEASED MATERIAL.

NO DIRECT INVITATION TO FOLLOW A PERSON.

NO CLAIM OF CLEANSING, REMOVAL, OR CONTACT WITHOUT ATTRIBUTION.

NO LIVE UPLOAD.

SESSION ENDS ON FACILITIES ORDER.

ALL ORIGINAL FILES PRESERVED.

Evan read the fifth line twice. “Direct invitation?”

“No language telling anything to enter a person, follow a person, use a person, speak through a person, or leave with a person.”

“Miriam doesn’t invite possession.”

“I have no accusation against her. The condition remains.”

“What does ‘without attribution’ mean?”

“You may record that Miriam says a room is clear. You may not state that the room became clear as an established fact.”

“What wording would you allow?”

“Completed ritual. Participants reported relief. No further activity observed during the remaining session.”

“That sounds like a maintenance report.”

“It is a record.”

He pointed at the board. “You left out closure.”

“Closure belongs to the person claiming it.”

Abby wrote the phrase in her notebook.

At 3:57, the service entrance buzzer sounded.

The noise came through Diane’s radio and the office wall at nearly the same moment. Abby raised the camera. Diane switched on the facilities recorder, stated the date only by case log number, named the location, listed the people present, and recorded the two device clocks. Evan reached for his larger camera.

“Production sources begin after visitor consent,” Diane said.

“They agreed by coming.”

“They agree after hearing the conditions.”

She led them upstairs.

Cold air moved through the service vestibule when the outer door opened. A woman in a charcoal wool coat stood beneath the security light with snow caught along one shoulder. She appeared near sixty, broad-faced and calm, with silver threaded through dark hair pulled loosely behind her head. Her gloves were ordinary leather. The canvas case in her hand could have held art supplies, medical equipment, or lunch. A younger woman stood behind her carrying a thermos and a folded blanket. Evan’s mother waited outside the glass, waving once before returning to her car.

“Miriam Vale,” the older woman said. “You must be facilities.”

“I’m Diane Madsen. Before you enter, I need your agreement to the session conditions.”

Miriam’s eyes moved briefly to Abby’s camera, then to the recorder in Diane’s hand. “Is that running?”

“Yes.”

“Good.”

Diane had prepared for an argument. Miriam agreed, so Diane had nothing to answer.

They entered the vestibule. The outer door closed, and the trapped air shifted hard enough to move the inner door against its closer. The younger woman caught it with one hand. Salt cracked beneath their shoes. Their coats began releasing cold into the overheated space.

Diane read every condition aloud.

Miriam listened without interruption. At the prohibition on student identities, she nodded. At the restriction against invitations, she asked Diane to repeat the wording, then said, “That is reasonable.” At the line concerning cleansing claims, she looked toward Evan.

“I can speak from my practice,” she said. “You can label it as my belief.”

“That is required,” Diane said.

“I need no certification from the school for what I perceive.”

Evan shifted the camera case higher on his shoulder. “The purpose is still to find out whether the activity changes.”

“The purpose,” Diane said, “is to document what occurs under stated conditions.”

Miriam smiled at neither of them. “Those can be different purposes in the same room.”

She spoke without theatrical emphasis. Her tone was gentle. Evan had no easy reply.

Diane asked, “What information have you received about Bellweather?”

“That students made a film. Staff reported sounds and movement in the basement and theater. Someone found an object where it was not expected. The investigation upset at least one employee. Evan’s mother believes the building needs peace.”

“Names?”

“Evan. Abby. No employee names.”

Evan faced the inner door.

“Historical identities?” Diane asked.

“None.”

“Deaths connected to the school?”

“None supplied.”

“Specific rooms?”

“Basement and auditorium. I saw the exterior photograph from the documentary page.”

Diane marked each exposure in Abby’s notebook while Abby filmed the page.

Miriam removed her gloves. “I can also leave.”

She offered without challenging Diane.

Diane looked through the vestibule window at the darkening service lot. Evan’s mother’s taillights had vanished beyond the snowbank. Inside, ordinary activity continued. A basketball struck the fieldhouse floor. The public-address system released a brief tone. Somewhere below, a relay changed state and sent a faint vibration through the wall.

Diane could still refuse.

Evan could also return later with weaker records, fewer witnesses, and no one authorized to end the session.

“You may enter under the written conditions,” Diane said.

Miriam inclined her head. “Then those are the conditions.”

Diane issued two visitor badges and recorded their numbers. The younger woman signed as Miriam’s assistant and gave her first name for the internal log. Abby synchronized the camera again. Diane held the facilities recorder close enough to capture the spoken time without turning the act into ceremony.

They moved toward the basement stair in a compact group: Diane first, Miriam behind her, Abby filming from the rear, Evan carrying the second camera, the assistant between them with the canvas case and thermos. The arrangement lasted until Evan stepped around Abby to point down the west corridor.

“That route reaches the auditorium,” he told Miriam. “Diane can show you the old booth later, but the sealed section stays off limits.”

Miriam turned her head toward him.

Diane stopped at the stair door.

Evan had spoken Diane’s name plainly. Every microphone was close enough to record it.

Evan showed no sign that he noticed. He adjusted the strap on his camera and reached for the push bar.

Abby’s eyes met Diane’s over the viewfinder.

Diane pressed the marker button on the facilities recorder.

Diane had yet to declare the formal start.

Every recorder already contained her name.

## Scene 2 — The First Room

Diane held the stair door while the others filed past her.

Evan went first despite the order she had given in the vestibule. He descended sideways to keep the camera aimed uphill, one hand on the rail and the other beneath the lens. Miriam followed him. Her assistant carried the canvas case against her hip. Abby moved behind them with the continuous-source camera, leaving Diane at the landing with the facilities recorder and a clear view of four backs.

Six steps down, Evan stopped beneath the first pipe crossing and pointed his camera toward a patch of flaking paint above the stairs. Miriam turned to see what he meant. Her assistant shifted around her. Abby stepped down to preserve the shot. Diane pulled the door closed before the closer struck her shoulder.

“Keep moving,” Diane said. “The landing has to remain clear.”

Evan resumed his descent. “This is where we heard movement during the first investigation. Ron was above us, and something came through the stairwell after he left.”

Diane pressed the marker button on her recorder.

“Second pre-session disclosure,” Abby said from behind Miriam. “Four-oh-six and twelve seconds. The first was Diane’s name.”

Evan glanced over his shoulder. “She already knows there were sounds.”

“She knows staff reported sounds,” Abby said. “She has not heard your sequence.”

“There’s a difference?”

“Yes.”

Miriam kept one gloved hand on the rail. “I would rather know what I hear before I know what you heard.”

Evan lowered the camera a few inches. “Fine.”

At the bottom, he pushed through the fire door and entered the first basement room. The others followed before Diane reached the threshold. The room had served as a storage area when the mechanical wing opened: painted block walls, a floor drain near the center, shelving anchored beneath a run of insulated pipe. Most of the shelves stood empty now. A stack of folded wrestling mats occupied one wall. The old service opening lay behind them, low and square, where two sections of shelving stopped short of the corner.

Diane entered last and closed the fire door until the latch caught.

“Positions,” she said.

Abby turned slowly with the camera and named each person in order. Evan stood near the drain. Miriam and her assistant were beside the north shelving. Diane remained at the door. Abby marked the room temperature from a pocket thermometer, then filmed the floor, ceiling, pipe runs, both doors, and the service opening.

Diane stated the time. “Formal session begins at four-oh-seven. The written conditions remain in force.”

Evan raised a handheld meter. Its green indicator lit beneath his thumb.

“Device operating,” Abby said. “Record the face and serial.”

He held it toward her lens. The display settled near zero.

